# python is one of the twelve grammars the board reads at 100.0% standing, and
# it resolves `print(x)` to Python 2's print STATEMENT.
#
#   outliner                      tree-sitter
#   print_statement [0, 8)        call [0, 8)
#     "print"                       identifier "print"
#     parenthesized_expression      argument_list
#       "(" identifier ")"            "(" identifier ")"
#
# Both parsers read the same `python.json`, and that grammar really does carry
# `print_statement` - tree-sitter-python keeps it for Python 2 sources. So this
# is not an invented node. It is an AMBIGUITY RESOLVED THE OTHER WAY: the same
# eight bytes derive through two live rules, and outliner takes the one that
# makes `print` a keyword rather than a name.
#
# The cost is not cosmetic. Under `print_statement`, `print` is not an
# identifier, so it is not a reference; `(x)` is a parenthesized expression
# rather than an argument list, so `x` is not an argument. Anything walking
# this tree for calls does not see one.
#
# 8 of 9 bytes: 5 askew (the `print` leaf itself changes kind) and 3 racked
# (the argument bytes, where both parsers agree leaf for leaf and disagree
# about what encloses them). `plumb` charges 5 of them.
roots 1
mends 0
holds call
holds argument_list
lacks print_statement
lacks parenthesized_expression
spans call 0 8
